# Local civil-date coverage: independent architecture review

**Recommendation:** specify a set of existing UTC intervals, with explicit empty and unresolved outcomes. Derive first/last instants from that set. Do not replace the current helper with another “resolve two midnights” implementation: real midnight-crossing folds disprove that contract, even when a standards-based start-of-day operation is used. Build the correction on one complete transition provider before advertising exact date membership; a denser Intl sample is a bounded approximation with a distinct qualification.

This is a read-only recommendation, not an implemented or accepted product change. Root owns integration, caller policy and the provider decision. No rc.6 adoption, repository mutation, publication or service activation was performed.

## Refreshed scope and executed evidence

The copied source identity records site HEAD `c761a49c55d125bca48ff38d81b0a9ff6fd5adcf` and SDK HEAD `ac27761e6dea138842e6ef5c2c69129ead7af636`. The current SDK has `resolveLocalToUtc`/`resolveBirth`, but **no localDateEndpointsUtc implementation, export or caller**. The site helper and its imported sources were copied before probing. The independent caller trace has 28 source hashes and identifies one production direct caller: ChartCalculator's unknown-time branch. Daily Sky uses explicit UTC dates and needs no migration.

The same 17 finite cases were executed in Node22.23.2 / ICU78.2 / tzdb2026a and native Chrome152.0.7977.83. Nine current-helper first/last results disagree with the reference interval sets in each runtime. Independent Gregorian wall formatting at every discovered segment's first/final millisecond agrees with offset arithmetic and the requested date. Chrome's actual Temporal transition enumeration agrees with all17 sets (29 transition queries); this cross-check shares the browser's timezone data and is **not independent historical timezone evidence**. Node22 has no native Temporal; Chrome152 does. No broader browser availability claim is made.

